  "text": "**Assessing the three answers**\n\n- **Solution 1 (1/(3L))**: The derivation is hand-wavy. It asserts a \"tight\" constant from a Young's-inequality bound without showing the bound is sharp, and the step from $\\mu^2(5+\\delta^{-1}+4\\delta)<1$ to $\\lambda L<1/3$ is not justified. I don't trust it.\n- **Solution 3 (1/L)**: The claim that $1/L$ is tight is not supported. The example $F(x)=Lx$ is monotone and linear, and a counterexample at exactly that scale would need checking. The stated literature citations are also vague. I don't trust it.\n- **Solution 2 (λ < 1/(2L))**: This matches the stepsize bound I recall for Popov's past-extragradient method with a constant stepsize, which is typically stated as $\\lambda < 1/(2L)$. Its tightness argument is sketchy, since it doesn't construct the counterexample. Still, the final value is the most plausible of the three.\n\nI can't verify this against the literature from here, so my confidence is moderate. Solution 2 is the best candidate, though its tightness claim is unproven.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
